          "source_quote": "τὸν ἐν ἀπορρήτοις αὐτοῦ πατέρα",
          "english_quote": "his Father in secrecy",
          "issue": "The Greek places the secrecy expression within the noun phrase describing the Father. The English readily makes it describe the manner of revealing instead, obscuring the source's attachment.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “his Father who is in secret” or another explicitly attributive formulation. Review the resulting sentence afresh.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἄνευ φωνῆς τῆς αὐτοῦ βουλῆς ἀποκαλυπτόμενον",
          "english_quote": "being uncovered without a voice by his will",
          "issue": "The main rendering gives τῆς αὐτοῦ βουλῆς independent causal or instrumental force: “by his will.” Its straightforward grammatical attachment is to φωνῆς, “a voice of his will.” Although the note discloses this attachment alternative, merely asserting another grouping does not establish the causal rendering from the supplied genitive. This is a translation-support problem, not evidence that the verified print is corrupt.",
          "suggested_revision": "For fresh review, consider “being uncovered without a voice expressing his will.” Alternatively, provide a specific grammatical justification for retaining “by his will” from the unchanged supplied Greek. Do not silently substitute a dative reading.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "18.7",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": false,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "Μὴ ἀπατῶ·",
          "english_quote": "Do not deceive:",
          "issue": "ἀπατῶ is first-person singular, not a second-person imperative. The draft turns Simon's first-person expression into a prohibition addressed to someone else. The possible indicative/interrogative and subjunctive construals of the supplied wording are not disclosed.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the first person. A proposed rendering is “Let me not deceive:” with a note explaining the first-person subjunctive construal and preserving the possible interrogative alternative, “Am I deceiving?” with its expectation of a negative answer. Context and the edition's printed stop should be discussed without treating ἀπατῶ as an OCR error or silently changing it.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
