      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "γνῶναι τὸν ἐν ἀπορρήτοις πατέρα",
          "english_quote": "to know the Father in secrecy",
          "issue": "As in 18.6, ἐν ἀπορρήτοις describes the Father within his noun phrase. The English can instead describe knowing him secretly. That distinction matters here because Simon immediately describes his disclosure as public.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “to know the Father who is in secret” or an equally clear attributive rendering, consistently with the corresponding phrase in 18.6.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "18.10",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τίνι γὰρ εὔλογόν ἐστιν ἀποκαλύπτειν τὸν πατέρα ἢ υἱῷ μόνῳ διὰ τὸ εἰδέναι τῆς τοιαύτης ἀποκαλύψεως τὸν ἄξιον;",
          "english_quote": "For to whom is it reasonable to reveal the Father, except the Son alone, because he knows the person worthy of such revelation?",
          "issue": "In context, τίνι and υἱῷ identify the person for whom revealing the Father is appropriate—the revealer—not the recipient of that revelation. The preceding appointment of the Son and the following reason, his knowledge of worthy recipients, establish this role. The English “to whom … reveal the Father” instead presents the Son as the recipient.",
          "suggested_revision": "For fresh review, use “For who can reasonably reveal the Father except the Son alone, since he knows who is worthy of such revelation?” This makes the Son the revealer while preserving the stated reason.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
